A||||3||ur2biteme||Replacing inner tie rods||||1||
Z||000000||ur2biteme||08-14-2000||05:26 AM||||i was wondering what exactly goes into replacing the inner tie rods on the 84-87 rack and pinion.  what special tools are needed, and what i'd need to replace the bushing you guys have been talking about.  i'm gonna try to not have to replace the rack if possible.  <P>The car seemed to have been well maintained most of it's life prior to the owners who ragged it out just prior to my purchase, so i'm not sure if the rack is bad, but i know the inner tie rods are. <P>any help is greatly appreciated<P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>165k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000001||fierospeeder||08-14-2000||10:54 PM||||i changed my inner tie rod because i accidently bent one by being stupid.<P>The way i did it, was i dropped the rack and pinion, there are 4 bolts holding it, and be carefull because i had two of them brake on me.  I had to take a air chisel and break the nuts off and a hammer and chisel to get the upper nuts off.  Also i had to disconnect the rod going to the steering wheel.<P>I used a two wrenches to get the inner tie rod off.<P>After i did my tie rod swap.  I was at Pep Boys and looked at the tool section.<P>Pep boys sells a foot long socket that fits over the tie rod and takes it off.<BR>Its about 20-30 bucks and i would get it next time i change mine.<BR>You will also need a special crimping pliers to crimp the metal band around the booty.  I think its called rabbit ears.  I got the wrong pliers.||||reg||1
Z||000002||TOM||08-16-2000||04:52 PM||||I changed one on my 86. Just pulled the boot back and undid the tie rod end. You need to turn the steering wheel all the way to get the nut end of the rod exposed.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||08-16-2000||08:57 PM||||No way do you need to pull the rack off to change inner tie rods. Yes it helps to have the special tool, but can be done ok without it. I used large worm-gear hose clamps to reinstall the rubber boots.||||reg||1
